# Preserve Existing Features

When implementing new features or making changes, existing functionality must not be broken or removed.

## Rules

1. **Never delete code that is unrelated to the current change.** If a block of code looks unused but is outside the scope of the task, leave it in place.
2. **Before removing any code**, verify it is truly unused or replaced:
   - Search for all call-sites / imports / references.
   - Confirm no other feature depends on it.
3. **Before overwriting a component, hook, or utility**, re-read the original file to understand all behaviors it provides — not just the one being changed.
4. **If a refactor requires removing code**, explicitly list what is being removed and why before proceeding.

## Examples

```
# ❌ BAD — Rewrites a component and silently drops an existing feature
- Replaced UserPanel component; the "export CSV" button was removed
  because the new implementation didn't account for it.

# ✅ GOOD — Adds new behavior while keeping existing features intact
- Extended UserPanel with new filtering controls.
- Preserved the existing "export CSV" button and verified it still works.
```

## Checklist (before submitting a change)

- [ ] All pre-existing features in modified files still function.
- [ ] No unrelated code was deleted or commented out.
- [ ] Any intentional removal is called out explicitly with justification.
